"""Original KS3 definition cards and retrieval checks in plain language."""
R={}
def pack(key,cards,q,correct,wrong1,wrong2,why):
 R[key]={'cards':[dict(term=t.split('=',1)[0],definition=t.split('=',1)[1]) for t in cards.split('~')], 'quiz':dict(q=q,opts=[correct,wrong1,wrong2],a=0,why=why)}
pack('safety','Hazard=Something that could cause harm.~Risk=The chance of harm and how serious it could be.','Which action protects your eyes from splashes?','Wear safety goggles.','Only write “be careful”.','Take no measurements.','Goggles help stop splashes reaching your eyes.')
pack('variables','Independent variable=The thing you choose to change in a test.~Dependent variable=The thing you measure in a test.~Control variable=Something you keep the same to make a test fair.','In a test of temperature and dissolving time, what do you measure?','Time taken for the solid to dissolve.','The temperature you choose.','The label on the beaker.','You change the temperature and measure the time taken to dissolve.')
pack('planning','Resolution=The smallest change a measuring instrument can show.~Repeatability=Getting similar results when the same person repeats the same test.','Why time ten swings instead of one?',"It reduces the effect of your reaction time on each swing's time.",'It fixes every equipment error.','It makes the clock perfect.',"Divide the longer total time by ten. Your reaction time then has a smaller effect on each swing's time.")
pack('burner','Combustion=Burning: a reaction with oxygen that transfers energy to the surroundings.~Safety flame=The visible yellow Bunsen flame used when you are not heating.',"What does opening a Bunsen burner's air hole change?",'The amount of air and oxygen entering.','The type of gas fuel.','The mass of the burner.','More oxygen helps the gas burn more completely, making a blue heating flame.')
pack('data','Continuous data=Measurements with values anywhere within a range, such as height.~Discrete data=Separate counts or values, such as the number of leaves.~Conclusion=An answer to your test question, supported by your results.','What makes a good conclusion?','Describe a pattern and use numbers from the results.','Choose your favourite result and ignore the others.','Leave units off the graph.','Numbers from the results give evidence for the pattern you describe.')
pack('evaluate','Random error=An unpredictable change between repeated measurements.~Systematic error=An error that makes readings wrong in the same way each time.~Anomaly=A result that does not fit the pattern.','A balance always reads 2 g too high. What should you do?','Check and correct its zero or calibration.','Just take a mean of more wrong readings.','Call it a random error.','A mean will still be too high. The balance needs correcting before new measurements are made.')
pack('microscope','Magnification=How many times bigger an image is than the real object.~Resolution=How well an image shows two close details separately.','What is the total magnification with a ×10 eyepiece and ×40 objective?','×400.','×50.','×4.','Multiply the lens magnifications: 10 × 40 = 400.')
pack('cell','Nucleus=The cell part containing genetic information.~Cell membrane=A thin boundary that controls what enters and leaves a cell.~Chloroplast=A cell part containing chlorophyll, which absorbs light for photosynthesis.','Which statement about plant cells is correct?','Most root cells have no chloroplasts.','Every plant cell has chloroplasts.','Plant cells do not respire.','Most root cells receive little light and do not carry out photosynthesis.')
pack('specialised','Specialised cell=A cell with features that help it do a particular job.~Unicellular organism=A living thing made of one cell.','Why does a root hair cell have a long, thin extension?','It gives a bigger surface area for taking in water and minerals.','It turns it into an animal cell.','It makes sunlight.','More surface area means more contact with the water around soil particles.')
pack('diffusion','Diffusion=Particles spreading from a higher concentration to a lower concentration.~Concentration gradient=A difference in concentration between two places.','What happens when diffusing particles are evenly spread?','They keep moving in both directions.','They all stop moving.','They only move towards the most crowded area.','Particles keep moving, but equal amounts move each way overall.')
pack('organisation','Tissue=A group of cells working together to do a job.~Organ=A body part made of different tissues working together.~Ligament=A strong band joining bone to bone.','What joins a muscle to a bone?','A tendon.','A ligament.','Only cartilage.','Tendons join muscle to bone. Ligaments join bone to bone.')
pack('muscles','Antagonistic pair=Two muscles that pull a joint in opposite directions.~Contraction=When a muscle develops a pulling force, often becoming shorter.','Why do two muscles work as an opposing pair at the elbow?','Muscles pull bones but cannot actively push them back.','A relaxed muscle pushes the bone back.','The bones contract.','One muscle pulls to bend the joint and the other pulls to straighten it.')
pack('particles','Solid=A state with close particles vibrating in fixed positions.~Liquid=A state with close particles that move past each other.~Gas=A state with widely spaced particles moving freely.','Why is a gas easy to squeeze into a smaller space?','There are large gaps between particles.','The particles shrink.','It contains no particles.','Squeezing a gas reduces the gaps between its particles.')
pack('state',"Melting=Changing from a solid to a liquid.~Evaporation=Particles leaving a liquid's surface as a gas.~Condensation=Changing from a gas to a liquid.",'How is evaporation different from boiling?','It can happen at the surface below the boiling point.','It turns water into a different element.','It must happen throughout the liquid.','Evaporation happens at the surface. Boiling happens throughout the liquid at its boiling point.')
pack('gas','Gas pressure=The force on each unit area caused by gas particles hitting a surface.~Compression=Squeezing something into a smaller volume.','What happens when you heat gas in a sealed container that cannot expand?','Its pressure rises.','Its number of particles falls.','Its particles stop hitting the walls.','The particles move faster and hit the walls more often and harder.')
pack('atoms','Element=A substance containing just one type of atom.~Compound=A substance with different elements chemically joined in a fixed ratio.~Mixture=Substances together without being chemically joined to each other.','Which substance is a compound?','Pure water.','Air.','Iron.','Water contains hydrogen and oxygen atoms chemically joined together.')
pack('solution','Solute=A substance that dissolves in a solvent.~Solvent=A liquid that dissolves a solute.~Saturated solution=A solution that cannot dissolve more solute at that temperature.','What does stirring usually increase?','How quickly a solid dissolves.','How much can dissolve at a fixed temperature.','The number of atoms in the solid.','Stirring brings fresh solvent to the solid. It helps dissolving happen faster.')
pack('filter','Residue=The solid left on filter paper.~Filtrate=The liquid that passes through filter paper.~Crystallisation=Making solid crystals from a solution.','Can ordinary filter paper remove dissolved salt from water?','No. Dissolved salt passes through with the water.','Yes. It traps all the dissolved salt.','Yes. It turns the salt into sand.','Filtering traps insoluble solids, such as sand. It does not trap dissolved salt.')
pack('distil','Distillation=Separating a liquid by evaporating it and then condensing its vapour.~Chromatography=Separating dissolved substances because they travel different distances in a solvent.','What does a condenser do during distillation?','Cools vapour so it becomes liquid.','Filters salt from water.','Makes new water molecules.','Cooling turns the vapour back into liquid so you can collect it.')
pack('energy','Energy store=A way energy is held, such as in a moving object or a battery.~Dissipation=Energy spreading into the surroundings and becoming less useful.','What happens to energy we call “wasted”?','It spreads into less useful stores, often in the surroundings.','It is destroyed.','It stops being conserved.','The energy still exists, but it is less useful for the job we wanted.')
pack('food-energy','Chemical energy store=Energy stored in substances such as food, fuels and batteries.~Energy per gram=The energy for each gram of a substance.','Why compare food energy per gram?','The food samples might have different masses.',"A water temperature rise measures all the food's energy perfectly.",'Every food contains the same energy.','Comparing equal masses makes the comparison fairer.')
pack('resources','Renewable resource=An energy resource that nature replaces as we use it.~Non-renewable resource=An energy resource used faster than nature replaces it.','Which is a renewable energy resource?','Wind.','Natural gas.','Uranium fuel.','Wind is replaced naturally, although it does not blow all the time.')
pack('power','Power=Energy transferred each second.~Kilowatt-hour=The energy transferred by a 1 kW device in one hour.','What is measured in kilowatt-hours, kWh?','Energy.','Power.','Current.','Power × time gives energy. A kilowatt alone measures power.')
pack('efficiency','Efficiency=The useful energy output divided by the total energy input.~Sankey diagram=A diagram with arrow widths showing amounts of energy transferred.','A device gives 20 J of useful energy from 100 J input. What is its efficiency?','20%.','80%.','500%.','20 ÷ 100 × 100 = 20%.')
pack('variation','Variation=Differences between living things of the same species.~Continuous variation=Differences with values across a range, such as height.~Discontinuous variation=Differences in separate groups, such as ABO blood group.','Which shows continuous variation?','Arm span.','ABO blood group.','Whether a feature is present or absent.','Arm span can have many measured values within a range.')
pack('adaptation','Adaptation=An inherited feature that helps a living thing survive and reproduce in its environment.~Selection pressure=Something in the environment that affects which organisms survive and reproduce.','Which statement about inherited adaptations is correct?','They can become more common over generations.','An animal chooses a new gene when it needs one.','They are helpful in every environment.','A helpful inherited feature can be passed on by survivors to their offspring.')
pack('puberty','Puberty=Body changes that prepare a child for adulthood and reproduction.~Gamete=A sex cell, such as a sperm or egg, with half the usual chromosome number.','Where are sperm produced?','In the testes.','In the uterus.','In the oviducts.','Testes produce sperm and ovaries produce egg cells.')
pack('cycle','Menstruation=When the uterus lining leaves the body during a period.~Ovulation=The release of an egg from an ovary.','Why does a 28-day diagram not describe every menstrual cycle?','Cycle length and the timing of ovulation vary.','Every cycle is exactly 28 days.','Ovulation is the same as a period.','A diagram shows a typical pattern. Real cycles can be shorter or longer.')
pack('pregnancy','Fertilisation=The joining of a sperm-cell nucleus and an egg-cell nucleus.~Implantation=An embryo attaching to the uterus lining.~Placenta=An organ that exchanges nutrients, oxygen and waste between the mother and developing baby.','Where does fertilisation usually happen in humans?','In an oviduct, also called a fallopian tube.','In the uterus lining during implantation.','In the placenta.','The embryo travels to the uterus and implants there after fertilisation.')
pack('flower','Pollination=Moving pollen from an anther to a stigma.~Anther=The part of a flower that makes pollen.~Stigma=The flower surface that receives pollen.','Which example is pollination?','Pollen landing on a suitable stigma.','Sperm and egg nuclei joining.','A seed starting to grow.','Pollination is moving pollen. Fertilisation is joining sex-cell nuclei later.')
pack('seed',"Seed dispersal=Spreading seeds away from the parent plant.~Ovule=A part inside the flower's ovary that can become a seed after fertilisation.",'Which flower part develops into a seed after fertilisation?','An ovule.','The whole anther.','A petal.','A fertilised ovule becomes a seed. The ovary often becomes a fruit.')
pack('germination','Germination=When a seed starts to grow into a seedling.~Control group=A comparison group used to help find the effect of the thing being tested.','What do most seeds need to germinate?','Water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.','Light in every case.','Soil as their food supply.','Seeds use stored food at first. Many can germinate without light.')
pack('reaction','Reactant=A starting substance in a chemical reaction.~Product=A new substance made in a chemical reaction.~Physical change=A change that does not make a new substance.','Which observation alone does not prove a chemical reaction?','Bubbles appearing.','Clear evidence of a new substance forming.','A test confirming a different chemical substance.','Boiling water also makes bubbles, without making a new substance.')
pack('acid','Acid=A substance that gives a solution with pH below 7 in water.~Alkali=A base that dissolves in water.~Concentration=How much dissolved substance there is in a given volume of solution.','What does “concentrated” tell you about an acid solution?','How much acid there is in a given volume of solution.','How much of the acid splits into charged particles.','That the acid must be strong.','Concentration describes the amount of acid. Strength describes a different property of the acid.')
pack('neutral','Neutralisation=A reaction between an acid and a base.~Salt=A type of compound made in reactions such as acid reacting with a metal oxide.','What type of salt does hydrochloric acid make?','A chloride.','A sulfate.','A nitrate.','Hydrochloric acid makes chlorides. Sulfuric acid makes sulfates.')
pack('metals','Displacement=A more reactive element replacing a less reactive element in a compound.~Oxidation=A reaction in which a substance gains oxygen, in these examples.','Iron is more reactive than copper. Can copper displace iron from iron sulfate?','No.','Yes, because all metals displace each other.','Yes, because copper is coloured.','The more reactive metal displaces the less reactive metal, not the other way around.')
pack('metal-acid','Reactivity series=A list of metals ordered by how readily they react.~Hydrogen test=A small gas sample makes a squeaky pop with a lighted splint in a teacher-supervised test.','What forms when magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid?','Magnesium chloride and hydrogen.','Magnesium sulfate and oxygen.','Only water.','A suitable metal and dilute acid make a salt and hydrogen gas.')
pack('charge','Electron=A tiny particle with a negative electric charge.~Static charge=A build-up of electric charge on an object.','An insulating rod gains electrons. What charge does it have?','Negative.','Positive.','Always zero.','Electrons are negative, so gaining extra electrons makes the rod negatively charged.')
pack('current','Current=The amount of electric charge flowing each second.~Ammeter=An instrument connected in series to measure current.','What happens to current at a junction?','Total current in equals total current out.','Current is used up.','Each branch always gets all the supply current.','Charge does not disappear. The branch currents add to the current before the split.')
pack('voltage','Potential difference=Energy transferred per coulomb (a unit of charge), also called voltage.~Resistance=How much a component limits current; calculated as voltage divided by current.~Voltmeter=An instrument connected across a component to measure voltage.','How should you connect a voltmeter?','In parallel across a component.','In series in place of a wire.','With only one lead connected.','Voltage is measured between two points, so the voltmeter goes across the component.')
pack('foodweb','Producer=A living thing that makes its own food, usually by photosynthesis.~Consumer=A living thing that gets food by eating other living things.~Food web=Several food chains linked together.','Which way does an arrow point in a food chain?','From the food towards the eater.','From the eater towards its food.','Always towards the producer.','Arrows show the direction that energy passes through the chain.')
pack('ecosystem','Ecosystem=Living things in an area and the surroundings they interact with.~Competition=Living things needing the same limited resource.~Quadrat=A frame marking a known area for counting plants or other organisms.','Why place quadrats at random points?','To avoid choosing only patches that support your prediction.','To make every count identical.','To remove the need for repeats.','Random points reduce the effect of the person choosing where to count.')
pack('speed','Average speed=Total distance travelled divided by total time.~Repeat=Taking another measurement using the same chosen conditions.','A trolley travels 6 m in 3 seconds. What is its average speed?','2 m/s.','18 m/s.','0.5 m/s.','Average speed = 6 ÷ 3 = 2 m/s.')
pack('dt','Gradient=The steepness of a graph line, found from vertical change divided by horizontal change.~Distance–time graph=A graph showing distance travelled as time passes.','What does a horizontal distance–time line show?','The object is stationary.','The object is speeding up.','The object is travelling uphill.','Time passes but distance does not change, so the object is not moving.')
pack('force','Resultant force=The overall force after combining all forces and their directions.~Balanced forces=Forces that cancel out to give zero overall force.','What can an object do when its forces are balanced?','Stay still or move steadily in a straight line.','Always speed up.','Only stay still.','Balanced forces do not change speed or direction. An object can already be moving.')
pack('gravity','Mass=The amount of matter in an object, measured in kilograms.~Weight=The force of gravity on an object, measured in newtons.~Terminal velocity=Steady falling speed reached when weight and drag balance.','At terminal velocity, how do drag and weight compare?','They are equal in size and opposite in direction.','Drag is zero.','Weight has disappeared.','Balanced forces mean the falling object stops speeding up.')
pack('crater','Model=A simpler version of something, used to explore an idea.~Control variable=Something kept the same to make a test fair.','When testing drop height and crater size, what should stay the same?','The ball and the type and depth of the surface material.','Drop height.','Nothing.','Keeping the ball and surface the same helps show the effect of drop height.')
pack('elements','Chemical symbol=The one- or two-letter label for an element.~Chemical formula=Symbols and numbers showing the atoms in a substance.','What does CO₂ show?','One carbon atom joined to two oxygen atoms.','Two carbon atoms joined to one oxygen atom.','A mixture of separate carbon and oxygen.','The small 2 refers to oxygen. No number after C means one carbon atom.')
pack('polymer','Polymer=A substance made of long molecules with repeating small parts.~Biodegradable=Able to be broken down by living things such as microorganisms in suitable conditions.','Which statement about polymers is correct?','Different polymers have different useful properties.','Every polymer is the same material.','Every polymer breaks down quickly in soil.','Choose a polymer whose properties suit the job, such as a flexible electrical insulator.')
pack('periodic','Group=A vertical column of the periodic table.~Period=A horizontal row of the periodic table.','Why is a periodic-table group useful?','Its elements often have similar chemical properties.','Every element in it has exactly the same mass.','Its elements are all the same substance.','Group patterns help predict properties and reactions.')
pack('groups','Halogen=A reactive non-metal in Group 7, also numbered Group 17.~Noble gas=A Group 0 element that rarely reacts, also numbered Group 18.','How does reactivity change down Group 1?','It generally increases.','It generally decreases.','Every element is unreactive.','The lower Group 1 metals generally react more readily.')
pack('transition','Catalyst=A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up overall.~Transition metal=A metal in the central part of the periodic table, such as iron or copper in school examples.','Why is copper useful for electric wires?','It conducts electricity well.','It is an electrical insulator.','It is a gas at room temperature.','Copper lets charge flow easily through a wire.')
pack('lungs','Ventilation=Moving air into and out of the lungs.~Alveolus=A tiny air sac in the lungs where gases move between air and blood.~Diaphragm=A sheet of muscle below the lungs that helps breathing.','What happens when you breathe in normally?','The diaphragm contracts and moves down.','The diaphragm moves up and chest space gets smaller.','Air is pushed out of the lungs.','The chest space gets larger, pressure falls and air moves in.')
pack('drugs','Drug=A substance that changes how the body works.~Dependence=Finding it difficult to stop using a substance, sometimes with withdrawal effects.','Which statement about nicotine vaping is correct?','It is not harmless and nicotine can cause dependence.','It is harmless for all young people.','Everyone who does not smoke should start vaping.','Vaping is unsuitable for children and non-smokers. Nicotine can make stopping difficult.')
pack('nutrition','Nutrient=A substance needed for energy, growth, repair or health.~Deficiency=Not getting enough of a needed nutrient.~Fibre=Material in food that we do not digest and that helps food move through the gut.','Why do you need iron in your diet?','To make haemoglobin for carrying oxygen in blood.','To replace all the water in the body.','To digest fibre into glucose.','Haemoglobin in red blood cells needs iron to carry oxygen.')
pack('food-tests',"Positive test=A result showing that the substance being tested for was detected.~Reducing sugar=A type of sugar, such as glucose, detected by Benedict's test with heating.",'What is a positive iodine test for starch?','The sample turns blue-black.','The sample stays orange-brown.','Bubbles prove starch is present.','Iodine turns blue-black when starch is detected.')
pack('digestion','Digestion=Breaking large food molecules into small molecules that can dissolve.~Enzyme=A substance made by living things that speeds up a chemical reaction.~Absorption=Digested nutrients passing through the gut wall.','How do villi help absorption?','They provide a larger surface area.','They make the intestine waterproof.','They stop nutrients reaching the blood.','More surface area lets nutrients pass into the body more quickly.')
pack('sound','Amplitude=The greatest distance a vibrating part moves from its resting position.~Frequency=The number of complete vibrations each second.~Longitudinal wave=A wave whose vibrations are along the direction it travels.','What changes when a sound has a higher pitch?','Its frequency is higher.','Its amplitude must be larger.','It must travel faster.','Pitch depends on frequency. Amplitude is linked to loudness.')
pack('ear','Echo=A sound that reflects from a surface.~Cochlea=The inner-ear part that turns vibrations into nerve signals.',"Why divide speed × echo time by two to find a wall's distance?",'The sound travels to the wall and back.','Sound only travels half the time.','The wall absorbs exactly half the sound.','The calculation first gives the total length of both journeys.')
pack('light','Normal=A line drawn at right angles to a surface where a ray hits it.~Reflection=A wave bouncing back from a surface.~Refraction=A wave changing direction as its speed changes between materials.','Where do you measure reflection angles from?','The normal.','The mirror surface.','Any line on the page.','The normal is at 90° to the surface. Both ray angles are measured from it.')
pack('colour','Filter=A material that lets some colours of light through and absorbs others.~Convex lens=A lens that can bring parallel light rays together.~Spectrum=A range of waves arranged by wavelength or frequency.','Why can a red object look dark in blue light?','There is no red light to reflect and it absorbs much of the blue.','It creates black light.','It must turn into a blue material.','An object can only reflect colours that are present in the light reaching it.')
pack('combustion','Thermal decomposition=Breaking a compound into simpler substances by heating it.~Complete combustion=A fuel burning fully with enough oxygen.','Which is thermal decomposition?','Heating calcium carbonate to make calcium oxide and carbon dioxide.','Melting ice.','Filtering sand from water.','A compound breaks down into new substances when heated.')
pack('mass','Closed system=A setup where no matter can enter or leave.~Conservation of mass=Total mass stays the same in a reaction when all matter is counted.','Why can an open flask lose measured mass during a reaction?','A gas leaves the flask.','Atoms are destroyed.','Mass stops existing.','The gas still exists outside the flask. Counting it would keep the total mass unchanged.')
pack('chemical-energy','Exothermic=Transferring energy to the surroundings overall, usually warming them.~Endothermic=Taking energy from the surroundings overall, usually cooling them.~Bond=A chemical link between atoms.','Which statement about bonds is correct?','Breaking bonds takes in energy and making bonds releases energy.','Breaking bonds always releases energy.','Making bonds always needs energy with none released.','Compare the energy taken in and released to decide whether the reaction heats or cools its surroundings.')
pack('catalyst','Activation energy=The energy needed to get a reaction started.~Catalyst=A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up overall.','What can a catalyst change when reactant amounts stay the same?','How quickly the products form.','The total number of starting atoms.','Whether atoms are conserved.','A catalyst can make the same amount of product form sooner.')
pack('respiration','Aerobic respiration=Releasing energy from glucose in cells using oxygen.~Respiration=Chemical processes in cells that release energy from food.','Which living things respire?','Plants and animals.','Only animals.','Only things that have lungs.','Plants and animals need energy for cell processes, so both respire.')
pack('anaerobic','Anaerobic respiration=Releasing energy from glucose without using oxygen.~Fermentation=Anaerobic respiration in yeast that makes ethanol and carbon dioxide.','Which gas makes bread dough rise?','Carbon dioxide.','Oxygen.','Nitrogen.','Yeast makes carbon dioxide bubbles that expand the dough.')
pack('photosynthesis','Photosynthesis=Using light to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen.~Chlorophyll=The green substance that absorbs light for photosynthesis.','Where does the carbon used to build much of a plant come from?','Carbon dioxide in the air.','Only minerals in the soil.','The light itself.','Photosynthesis uses carbon dioxide to make glucose that can be used for growth.')
pack('leaf','Stoma=A tiny pore in a leaf that lets gases pass in and out.~Limiting factor=The thing in short supply that is holding back the rate.','Why can extra light stop making photosynthesis faster?','Another factor, such as carbon dioxide, is now in short supply.','The plant has stopped needing all energy.','Light has turned into soil minerals.','Extra light does not fix a shortage of carbon dioxide or an unsuitable temperature.')
pack('plant-practical','Proxy=A measurement used to estimate something else.~Destarching=Keeping a plant in the dark long enough for stored starch to be used up.','Why can gas volume be better than a bubble count?','Bubbles can have different sizes.','Every bubble has the same volume.','Bubble counts measure every oxygen molecule.','Counting ten large bubbles and ten small bubbles does not give the same gas volume.')
pack('investigate-candle','Independent variable=The thing you choose to change in a test.~Endpoint=The clear event that tells you when to stop measuring.','What should stay the same in a candle and container-volume test?','The candle and the rule for when timing stops.','The container volume.','Nothing.','Keep other things the same so you can compare the effect of container volume.')
pack('investigate-bounce','Rebound height=The highest point a ball reaches after a bounce.~Dissipation=Energy spreading to the surroundings and becoming less useful.','Why does a ball usually bounce lower than its drop height?','Some energy transfers to heating and sound.','Energy is destroyed.','Gravity switches off during the bounce.','Less energy remains for lifting the ball, but total energy is still conserved.')
pack('investigate-bungee','Extension=Stretched length minus original length.~Elastic deformation=A change of shape that reverses when the force is removed.','A cord stretches from 20 cm to 28 cm. What is its extension?','8 cm.','28 cm.','48 cm.','Extension is the extra length: 28 − 20 = 8 cm.')
pack('investigate-conduction','Thermal conductor=A material that transfers energy easily by conduction.~Fair comparison=A test keeping other factors the same so one change can be compared.','What must you control when comparing metal rods?','Rod size and the heating method.','Nothing.','Only the colour of the table.','A size or heating difference could change the result, even without changing the metal.')
pack('investigate-cupcake','Drag=A force that opposes movement through air or water.~Terminal velocity=Steady falling speed when weight and drag balance.','Does one average falling speed prove terminal velocity was reached?','No. Speed could have changed during the timed part.','Yes, every average is a terminal velocity.','Yes, if the object is made of paper.','Compare speeds in different parts of the fall to check whether speed became steady.')
pack('investigate-salt','Solubility=The maximum amount that dissolves in a given amount of solvent at a set temperature.~Dissolving rate=How quickly a substance dissolves.','What happens if extra salt is added to a saturated solution?','Some salt remains undissolved.','All extra salt dissolves instantly.','The salt becomes a different element.','No more can dissolve at that temperature, so some stays as a solid.')
pack('investigate-diffusion','Diffusion=Particles spreading from higher to lower concentration.~Convection=Energy transfer as a liquid or gas moves from place to place.','What else can spread dye in a warm-water test besides diffusion?','Convection currents moving the coloured water.','The dye turning into light.','The water particles becoming larger.','Moving water carries dye with it, so the test does not show diffusion alone.')
pack('magnet','Magnetic field=The region where magnetic forces act.~Induced magnet=A material that becomes magnetic near another magnet.','Which is better evidence that an object is a magnet?','It repels one pole of a known magnet.','It is attracted to a magnet.','It looks shiny.','Unmagnetised iron can be attracted, but repulsion shows the object has a like magnetic pole.')
pack('electromagnet','Electromagnet=A magnet made by an electric current in a coil.~Relay=A switch operated by an electromagnet.','How can you make an electromagnet stronger?','Increase current or add more turns under fair conditions.','Remove all the turns.','Switch the current off.','More current and more coil turns can make a stronger field. Control other factors when testing one change.')
pack('work','Work done=Energy transferred when a force moves something through a distance.~Simple machine=A device that can reduce a force by making it act over a greater distance.','A perfect pulley halves the lifting force. What else changes?','You pull the rope twice as far.','You get twice as much energy for free.','The load has no weight.','A smaller force acts over a longer distance. The machine does not create energy.')
pack('moments','Moment=The turning effect of a force around a pivot.~Pivot=The point an object turns around.','Which distance is used to calculate a moment?',"The right-angle distance from the pivot to the force's line.",'Any sloping distance from the pivot.','The total length of the object in every case.','Moment uses the perpendicular, or right-angle, distance.')
pack('thermal','Temperature=A measure of how hot or cold something is.~Conduction=Energy transfer through a material without the material flowing as a whole.','Must a hot small cup have more thermal energy than a cooler bath?','No. The mass of water also matters.','Yes. Temperature is the only factor.','Yes. Cups always hold more water than baths.','A bath can have a larger energy store because it holds much more water.')
pack('convection','Convection=Energy transfer by the movement of a liquid or gas.~Thermal radiation=Energy transfer by infrared waves.~Insulator=A material or arrangement that slows energy transfer.','Which method can transfer energy across empty space?','Radiation.','Convection.','Conduction.','Radiation needs no particles between objects. Conduction and convection do.')
pack('atmosphere','Atmosphere=The gases around a planet.~Carbon cycle=Carbon moving between living things, air, water and rocks.','Which process removes carbon dioxide from the air?','Photosynthesis.','Burning fuels.','Aerobic respiration.','Plants take in carbon dioxide and use it to make glucose.')
pack('climate','Greenhouse gas=A gas that absorbs infrared radiation and sends it out in all directions.~Climate=The pattern of weather over many years.','How do extra greenhouse gases increase warming?','They absorb more outgoing infrared radiation and send some back towards the surface.','They make a hole in the ozone layer as the main cause.','They create sunlight.',"More energy is kept in Earth's surface and atmosphere until a new balance is reached.")
pack('extraction','Ore=Rock containing enough useful material to make extraction worthwhile.~Reduction=Removing oxygen from a metal oxide in these examples.','Why is carbon not normally used to extract aluminium from its oxide?','Aluminium is more reactive than carbon.','Aluminium is less reactive than every metal.','Carbon can extract every metal.','Carbon cannot remove the oxygen from aluminium oxide by the usual carbon extraction method.')
pack('recycle','Reuse=Using an item again.~Recycling=Processing waste materials to make new products.~Life cycle=The stages from getting raw materials to making, using and disposing of a product.','Which statement about metal recycling is fair?','It saves ores but collection and processing still use resources.','It uses no energy at all.','It has no benefits.','Recycling can save ores and energy, but transport and processing still have costs.')
pack('selection','Natural selection=Helpful inherited features becoming more common through survival and reproduction over generations.~Inherited variation=Differences passed on through genes from parents to offspring.','What is needed for natural selection to change a population?','Inherited differences that affect survival and reproduction.','All individuals being exactly the same.','Animals choosing new genes when needed.','Survivors can pass helpful inherited features to their offspring.')
pack('evidence-evolution','Scientific theory=An explanation supported by evidence and testing.~Acquired characteristic=A feature gained during a lifetime, such as a scar.','Why is muscle training not the same as inherited evolution?','The larger trained muscles are not directly passed on in genes.','All exercise creates a new species.','Children inherit every change their parents experience.','Training changes an individual. Inherited evolution involves changes passed on across generations.')
pack('biodiversity','Biodiversity=The variety of living things, including different species and inherited differences.~Extinction=When the last living member of a species dies.~Conservation=Protecting living things and their habitats.','Why might captive breeding alone fail to save a species?','The animals still need a safe, suitable wild habitat.','Habitat never matters.','All released animals are guaranteed to survive.','Breeding more animals cannot by itself fix habitat loss or other threats in the wild.')
pack('inheritance','Gene=A section of DNA carrying information that helps control how cells work or features develop.~Allele=A different version of a gene.~Chromosome=A long, packaged DNA molecule.','What is an allele?','A version of a gene.','A whole animal cell.','A bone in a joint.','A gene can have different versions called alleles.')
pack('dna-practical','DNA=The molecule that carries genetic information.~Precipitate=A solid that comes out of a solution.','What are visible clumps in a classroom DNA extraction?','Many DNA molecules collected together, with possible other material.','One clearly visible double helix.','A complete living organism.','One DNA molecule is too small to see like this. Many collected molecules form the visible clumps.')
pack('cross','Genotype=The allele combination, such as Aa.~Phenotype=The feature that results from genes and the environment.~Recessive allele=An allele whose feature shows only with two copies in a simple dominant model.','For Aa × Aa in a simple dominant model, what is the chance of aa?','1 in 4.','1 in 2.','Certain.','The four possible combinations are AA, Aa, Aa and aa.')
pack('gm','Genetic modification=Changing DNA using laboratory techniques.~Selective breeding=Choosing parents with useful inherited features and breeding them over generations.','How is selective breeding different from genetic modification?','It chooses parents instead of directly changing their DNA in a laboratory.','It always moves a gene between unrelated species.','It needs no parents or offspring.','Breeders choose which organisms reproduce. Genetic modification directly changes genetic material.')
pack('waves','Transverse wave=A wave whose vibrations are across the direction it travels.~Wavelength=The distance between matching points on neighbouring waves.~Period=The time for one complete wave or vibration.','A wave has frequency 3 Hz and wavelength 2 m. What is its speed?','6 m/s.','1.5 m/s.','5 m/s.','Wave speed = frequency × wavelength = 3 × 2 = 6 m/s.')
pack('ultrasound','Ultrasound=Sound with a frequency above about 20,000 Hz.~Time of flight=The time a signal takes to travel a particular distance.','Can ultrasound travel across empty space?','No. Sound needs a material to travel through.','Yes, because it is light.','Yes, because it has a high pitch.','Ultrasound is still sound. It needs particles to pass on the vibrations.')
pack('em','Electromagnetic wave=A wave, such as light or radio, that can travel through empty space.~Ionisation=An atom or particle becoming charged by losing or gaining electrons.','Which electromagnetic waves travel at the same speed in a vacuum?','All types of electromagnetic wave.','Only visible colours.','Only radio and sound.','All electromagnetic waves have the same vacuum speed. Sound is not an electromagnetic wave.')
pack('earth',"Crust=Earth's thin, solid outer layer.~Mantle=The mostly solid rock layer below the crust that can slowly change shape.~Igneous rock=Rock made when molten rock cools and becomes solid.",'Which statement about Earth is correct?','The mantle is mostly solid and the outer core is liquid.','The whole mantle is liquid magma.',"The crust is Earth's thickest layer.",'The mantle is mostly solid rock, even though it can move very slowly over long times.')
pack('sediment','Weathering=Rock breaking down where it is.~Erosion=Rock material being removed and carried away.~Fossil=Preserved remains or signs of a past living thing.','How is erosion different from weathering?','Erosion carries material away; weathering breaks it down in place.','They always mean exactly the same thing.',"Erosion only happens inside Earth's core.",'A river carrying broken rock is erosion. Cracking rock in place is weathering.')
pack('rock-cycle','Metamorphic rock=Rock changed by heat and pressure without melting.~Rock cycle=The processes that change rocks from one type into another.','What forms when molten rock cools and becomes solid?','Igneous rock.','Only a fossil.','Liquid sediment.','Igneous rocks form by cooling and solidifying molten rock.')
pack('space','Light-year=The distance light travels in one year.~Solar System=The Sun and the objects that orbit it.~Galaxy=A huge collection of stars, gas and other matter held together by gravity.','What does a light-year measure?','Distance.','Time.','Mass.','The word “year” is part of the name, but a light-year is a distance.')
pack('orbits',"Orbit=The path of one object around another under gravity.~Axial tilt=The lean of a planet's spin axis compared with its orbit.","What mainly causes Earth's seasons?","Earth's tilted axis.",'A large yearly change in Earth–Sun distance.','The Moon making its own light.','The tilt changes day length and how directly sunlight reaches each hemisphere.')
pack('moon',"Phase=The amount of the Moon's sunlit half that we can see.~Lunar eclipse=The Moon passing through Earth's shadow.~Solar eclipse=The Moon blocking sunlight from reaching part of Earth.",'What causes normal Moon phases?','We see different amounts of the sunlit half as the Moon orbits Earth.',"Earth's shadow covers it every night.",'The Moon switches its own light on and off.','The Moon reflects sunlight. Its orbit changes our view of its lit half.')
pack('friction','Friction=A contact force that opposes sliding between surfaces.~Drag=A force opposing movement through air or water.~Plastic deformation=A permanent change of shape.','Why does a cyclist need a driving force at steady speed?','To balance friction and air resistance.','Because balanced forces must make the cyclist stop.','Because the bicycle has no weight.','A forward driving force balances the backward forces, giving no change in speed.')
pack('spring',"Spring constant=How much force is needed for each metre of extension while the spring follows Hooke's law.~Limit of proportionality=The point where force and extension stop following a straight-line pattern.",'Which length goes into force = spring constant × extension?','The extra length beyond the original length.','The total spring length.','The original length alone.','Extension = stretched length − original length.')
pack('pressure','Pressure=Force spread over an area, calculated as force divided by area.~Pascal=One newton of force per square metre.','What happens to pressure if area doubles but force stays the same?','It halves.','It doubles.','It stays the same.','The same force is spread over twice the area, so pressure is half as large.')
pack('liquid-pressure','Hydraulic system=A system using liquid to pass on a pressure change.~Liquid pressure=The force from a liquid on each unit area.','Why can a larger hydraulic output piston give a bigger force?','The same pressure acts on a larger area.','The liquid creates extra energy.','A bigger piston must move further than a smaller one.','Force = pressure × area. The bigger force is balanced by a shorter movement.')
